$DIR/main.p:14:1: //pu:extern function takeOwned: parameter 1: owned[main.Node] has no C equivalent; pass unsafe.Pointer(b) and keep the box on the Plutonium side
    Functions marked //pu:extern or //pu:export are called with the C calling
    convention. Their parameters and results must be types C has: integers,
    floats, bools, pointers, and structs and arrays of those. Strings, slices,
    interfaces, func values, maps, channels, rings and unions have no C
    equivalent, and variadic functions are not supported. Neither do owned,
    shared, synced and weak references: C cannot keep a count, drop an owner or
    check a generation, so a reference C kept or returned would be one no count
    knows about. This holds inside structs passed by value too.
    Pass a pointer and a length instead of a string or slice, and
    unsafe.Pointer(b) instead of a box, keeping the box on the Plutonium side
    while C may use the address.
    Pass a pointer and a length:
        //pu:extern write
        func write(fd int32, buf *byte, n uint64) int64

        write(1, unsafe.SliceData(b), uint64(len(b)))
    (see "Assembly and C" in the Plutonium spec, section 20.4)
    (run "pluto explain E2511" for a longer explanation)

